WEBVTT

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:09.450
A running lab is also a network. This is the netlab: a pfSense router, a Pi-hole and a client on a private segment.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:20.264
Map draws the topology live: the host with its forwards, the machines, the isolated segment below.

00:00:20.364 --> 00:00:27.779
Cables light up only with measured traffic, and every port shows its bytes per second.

00:00:27.879 --> 00:00:37.650
The lens on a cable opens the packet inspector: every row is a frame seen on that cable, with protocol, addresses and ports.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:48.547
Let us make a web request from the client to the Pi-hole, from the host terminal.

00:00:48.647 --> 00:00:54.193
There it is on the cable: TCP towards port 80, as it happens.

00:00:54.293 --> 00:01:02.406
The filters narrow the list: only TCP, or only what mentions port 80.

00:01:02.506 --> 00:01:06.515
Pause freezes the list; the capture goes on.

00:01:06.615 --> 00:01:20.732
A click on a row opens the packet: Ethernet, IP, TCP, field by field, and the bytes of its headers. The content is never recorded.

00:01:20.832 --> 00:01:29.646
The same for every lab, and for two machines joined with vmctl link.
